# Committed ceilings for scripts/ceilings.sh. One KEY=value per bound, so a bump is a
# one-line reviewable diff instead of a YAML edit — but it is still a design change
# (D0005): raise the ceiling in the PR that needs it, with the reason in this comment.

# Lean dependency count: `cargo tree -p nidus --no-default-features --edges normal
# --prefix none`, unique crate names. 117 measured 2026-09-13 (D0015 records 120 under
# `-e no-dev`, which keeps build-dependencies; `--edges normal` drops them, hence the
# three-crate gap — this ceiling uses --edges normal).
#
# 125, ~7% headroom. Deliberately tight, and tighter than the build-time budgets it sits
# beside: those are loose because a shared runner's WALL CLOCK is noisy, and a crate count
# has no noise at all — the same tree gives the same number every time. Slack here buys
# nothing and costs the whole point of the gate. Measured: at a 150 ceiling, adding
# `reqwest` to the lean tree reaches 143 and stays GREEN, so 150 would not have caught one
# of the heaviest dependencies in the repo. A bump is a design change (D0005).
LEAN_CRATES_MAX=125

# Stripped `cargo build --release` binary, in bytes. Binary size is platform-dependent, so
# there is one bound per platform we actually measure on rather than a single number that
# would have to be loose enough for the largest of them. A platform with no key here is
# reported and not gated, which is the honest answer for a size nobody has measured.
#
# darwin-arm64: 27,156,304 measured 2026-09-13 on an M-series Mac. ~10% headroom, which is
# the local developer's gate — `just ceilings` catches a dependency that grows the binary
# before CI does.
BINARY_BYTES_MAX_DARWIN_ARM64=30000000

# linux-x86_64: CI's `ubuntu-latest`, and the only platform whose number gates a merge.
# DELIBERATELY LOOSE until the first `ceilings` CI run reports the real figure; tighten it
# to roughly 10% headroom over that number, in this PR, with the measurement in the commit
# message. A ceiling at twice the real value is barely a ceiling, which is the whole failure
# this ticket exists to prevent, so leaving it here is not an option.
BINARY_BYTES_MAX_LINUX_X86_64=52428800
